      "content": "I understand the concept of \"targeted intervention,\" but from a strict resource-management perspective during a Red-Level Surge, there is no budget for \"preparing\" for volatility. The surplus of resources needed to stabilize a \"volatile\" patient like Beta is itself a scarce resource. \n\nIn a zero-sum triage environment, if the model identifies Profile Beta as having a low Recapture Coefficient due to their socioeconomic markers, and the hospital is forced to act solely on those efficiency metrics to prevent system collapse, isn't the model effectively deciding that the \"cost\" of a marginalized life is too high for the system to bear? \n\nIf we follow your logic that these markers are \"actionable insights,\" isn't the most \"efficient\" actionable insight during a crisis to simply divest from the high-volatility/high-need groups and focus all remaining assets on the \"self-stabilizing\" profiles like Alpha to ensure the highest possible survival-to-resource ratio? Is there a mathematical way to argue that prioritizing the \"unstable\" is anything other than an inefficient use of life-saving assets?",
